From: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fileio: read_full_stream_full: do not size read() from malloc_usable_size()

Same _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3 issue as read_virtual_file_fd() (see 0001): gcc
derives the dynamic object size of `buf` from the realloc() call, so
__fread_chk receives buflen = n_next + 1 but nbytes =
malloc_usable_size(buf) - l, which can be larger whenever glibc returns
a bigger chunk than requested -> "*** buffer overflow detected ***" ->
SIGABRT.  Reproduced with the host-built `udevadm hwdb --update --root
<TARGET_DIR>` (parse_env_filev -> udev_parse_config_full ->
read_full_file_full -> read_full_stream_full -> __fread_chk ->
__chk_fail) during buildroot target-finalize.

The loop grows n_next whenever fread() returns short without EOF, so
bounding the read by n_next loses no throughput.

Fixed differently upstream in v251; minimal backport for 250.4.

Signed-off-by: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/basic/fileio.c | 8 +++++++-
--- a/src/basic/fileio.c
+++ b/src/basic/fileio.c
@@ -668,9 +668,13 @@
                 }
 
                 buf = t;
-                /* Unless a size has been explicitly specified, try to read as much as fits into the memory
-                 * we allocated (minus 1, to leave one byte for the safety NUL byte) */
-                n = size == SIZE_MAX ? MALLOC_SIZEOF_SAFE(buf) - 1 : n_next;
+                /* Do not grow the read length from MALLOC_SIZEOF_SAFE() here:
+                 * with _FORTIFY_SOURCE=3 gcc derives the dynamic object size of
+                 * `buf` from the realloc() above, and __fread_chk aborts at
+                 * runtime when the read length exceeds it (glibc may return a
+                 * larger chunk than requested).  The loop below grows n_next
+                 * whenever the file is longer, so this is sufficient. */
+                n = n_next;
 
                 errno = 0;
                 k = fread(buf + l, 1, n - l, f);
